Titre$ D E C O N I C A    -    NOORDELOS (2011)
Com$ STROPHARIACEAE  -  Adapté par Paul Bertéa 31-10-2013
1,2 $ Fruit bodies pleurotoid with eccentric stipe or stipe lacking
1,3 $ Fruit bodies agaricoid with well-developed centrally inserted, stipe

2,0 $ Pileus translucently striate, with 60-90 µm thick gelatinous layer; growing on grasses, rushes, and sedges $ D. phillipsii
2,0 $ Pileus leathery, not translucently striate, without gelatinous layer; growing on wood $ D. horizontalis

3,4 $ Growing on soil, humus, peat, mosses, etc
3,22 $ Growing on dung (fimicolous)

4,5 $ Spore length > 10 µm on average
4,7 $ Spore length < 9 µm on average

5,0 $ Spores oblong-subfusiform, 9-12 x 4-6 µm, Q = 1.6-2.2; thin-walled with indistinct germ pore $ D. subfusispora
5,6 $ Spores ovoid to ellipsoid, Q = 1.4-1.8, thick-walled with distinct germ pore

6,0 $ Pileus viscid with a thick, peeling pellicle, indistinctly translucently striate; without any trace of veil $ D. pratensis
6,0 $ Pileus dry, pileipellis not peeling, translucently striate up to centre; at margin with scanty cortina $ D. montana var. macrospora

7,8 $ Spores thin- or slightly thick-walled; lamellae of fresh and dried specimens ochre-brown to red-brown, lacking a purple-brown or chocolate brown tinge $$s"subsection Inquilinae"
7,12 $ Spores with thickened walls; lamellae often dark purple-brown to chocolate brown in fresh and dried specimens $$s"subsection Deconica"

8,9 $ Pileus viscid; pileipellis entirely peeling off as a hyaline, gelatinous pellicle; spores often more or less rhomboid or subangular in frontal view
8,10 $ Pileus dry; pileipellis not or only slightly peeling off; spores never tending to become rhomboid or subangular in frontal view
8,10,N$/// ===> If spores to become rhomboid or subangular in frontal viewcompare D. subhyperella from S. America

9,0 $ Spores large, in average 7.8-8.6 x 5.9-6.1 x 5.0-5.3 µm, Qf = 1.4-1.5, Qs = 1.5-1.7; veil usually absent in mature basidiocarp; on dead leaves of grasses $ D. inquilina
9,0 $ Spores small, in average 6.0-7.5 x 4.6-5.1 x 4.0-5.3 µm, Qf = 1.3-1.6, Qs = 1.3-1.7; veil usually well-developed as appendiculate flocks at pileus margin and an annuliform zone and white fibrils on stipe; usually on woody-substrates $ D. crobula

10,11 $ Veil abundant, covering the young pileus entirely and visible as a cortina connecting the margin of the pileus with the stipe, leaving appendiculate patches at pileus margin in mature specimen; spores never strongly flattened, F/l << 50%
10,0 $ Veil usually only present as a cortina in young specimens, almost or entirely lacking in mature specimens $ D. subviscida var. subviscida

11,0 $ Spores ellipsoid to oblong in side view with small germ-pore; margin of pileus often grooved; in meadows, usually at base of grasses $ D. castanella
11,0 $ Spores amygdaliform with apical papilla, with large germ-pore, often somewhat truncate and flattened; margin of pileus never grooved; in marshy places, in clumps of Carex $ D. velifera

12,13 $ Spores rhomboid, F/l up to 90 %
12,16 $ Spores not rhomboid, F/l up to 50 % or much lower

13,14 $ Pileus viscid with (partly) peeling pellicle
13,15 $ Pileus dry, fibrillose to flocculose; pellicle not peeling

14,0 $ Veil absent; spores (5.5-) 6.0-7.5 (-8.0) x (4.5-) 5.0-6.5 (-7.0) x 4.0-4.5 µm $ D. phyllogena
14,0 $ Veil conspicuous in young specimens, appendiculate at margin of pileus in mature specimens; spores (7.5-) 9-10 (-11) x (5-) 6-7 (-8) µm $ D. submaritima

15,0 $ Pleurocystidia present, similar to cheilocystidia $ D. flocculosa
15,0 $ Pleurocystidia absent $ D. cf. rhomboidospora

16,0 $ Spores without or with a very small germ-pore $ D. micropora
16,17 $ Spores with a distinct germ-pore

17,0 $ Spores small, 5.5-7.0 x 4.0-5.0 (-5.5) x 3.5-4.5 µm $ D. xeroderma
17,18 $ Spores 7.0-9.5 µm long

18,19 $ Veil absent, or only a fugacious cortina present; usually among mosses on open, short vegetation types
18,20 $ Veil present as appendiculate patches at pileus margin and as fibrils and/or a annuliform zone on stipe; usually in mesic to eutrophic grasslands or in swampy areas among Juncus or Carex species

19,0 $ Spores in average 7.5-8.6 x 5.1-5.7 x 4.8-5.7 µm, Qf = 1.4-1.6, slightly flattened, ovoid to mitriform in side-view, often truncate because of large germ-pore; saprotrophic among mosses such as Polytrichum piliferum, Rhacomitrium canescens or Campylopus introflexus $ D. montana var. montana
19,0 $ Spores in average 7.3-8.4 x 4.9-5.5 x 5.7-5.9 µm, Qf = 1.3-1.55, flattened, appearing mitriform or slightly angular in frontal view, with medium-sized germ-pore; often in alpine snow-bed vegetation, parasitic on Polytrichum norvegicum but also found in lowland Fagus forest $ D. chionophila

20,0 $ Cheilocystidia 20-44 x 3.0-7.0 µm, lageniform or subcylindrical with long, attenuated, 1.5-3.0 µm wide neck; in groups on raw humus in fertilised grasslands, lawns, road-sides, also on rotten manure, straw or old dung $ D. subviscida var. velata
20,21 $ Cheilocystidia 20-35 µm long, either distinctly tibiiform or lageniform with relatively short neck; on damp soil in marshy places, wet meadows among Juncus or Carex, etc

21,0 $ Cheilocystidia predominantly tibiiform with distinct globose capitulum, 2-5 µm wide; spores moderately thick-walled, hardly flattened; stipe reddish brown in lower half, more or less glabrous $ D. magica
21,0 $ Cheilocystidia lageniform with relatively short neck, 3-6 µm wide; spores relatively thick-walled, often slightly to distinctly flattened; stipe blackish in lower part, with white fibrils $ D. schoeneti

22,23 $ Veil absent
22,24 $ Veil present in form of an annulus or annuliform zone on stipe and flocks on pileus

23,0 $ Spores hexagonal in frontal view, 10-14 µm long $ D. coprophila
23,0 $ Spores oblong in frontal view, (14.5-) 15-19 µm long $ D. subcoprophila

24,25 $ Spores hexagonal in frontal view
24,26 $ Spores ellipsoid to oblong in frontal view

25,0 $ Spores 11-12.5 x 7.5-9.0 x 7.0-8.0 µm; annulus fibrillose, often rather low on stipe $ D. merdaria
25,0 $ Spores 13-17 x 8.5-11 x 7.5-9.5 µm; annulus membranaceous, rarely fibrillose, rather high on stipe $ D. moelleri

26,0 $ Pileus yellow-brown, often with olivaceous tinges $ D. merdicola
26,0 $ Pileus dark brown to grey-brown
26,0,N$/// ===> compare Psilocybe fimetaria.
